          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Feet primarily signify a person’s walk, path, or moral conduct, though Scripture also uses them in several context-dependent ways.\",\"book_summary\":\"Feet signify a person’s walk or conduct, particularly the aspect that must be kept clean.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"A person’s practical or moral walk and conduct.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"A person’s walk or conduct.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The book emphasizes cleanliness within the same conduct metaphor rather than narrowing or replacing its referent.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book’s identification directly matches the consensus’s primary meaning.\",\"rationale\":\"After reducing both readings to their minimum cores, each identifies feet with a person’s walk or conduct. The book’s phrase “the way that must remain clean” adds a cleansing emphasis consistent with the consensus’s moral-conduct and footwashing strands, but does not materially alter which cases the symbol covers.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"John 13:10 explicitly concerns washing feet and being clean, but it does not itself explicitly state that feet symbolize conduct; that identification depends on a broader symbolic interpretation of the footwashing scene.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
